<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Shirley_MacLaine> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Shirley MacLaine** (born **Shirley MacLean Beaty**, April 24, 1934) is an American actress, author, and former dancer. Known for her portrayals of quirky, strong-willed and eccentric women, MacLaine has received [numerous accolades](:List_of_awards_and_nominations_received_by_Shirley_MacLaine) over her seven-decade career, including an [Academy Award](:Academy_Award), an [Emmy Award](:Primetime_Emmy_Award), two [British Academy Film Awards](:British_Academy_Film_Awards), six [Golden Globe Awards](:Golden_Globe_Awards), two [Volpi Cups](:Volpi_Cup_for_Best_Actress) and two [Silver Bears](:Silver_Bear_for_Best_Actress). MacLaine is one of the last remaining stars from the [Golden Age of Hollywood](:Classical_Hollywood_Cinema).Born in [Richmond, Virginia](:Richmond%2C_Virginia), MacLaine made her acting debut as a teenager with minor roles in the Broadway musicals *[Oklahoma!](:Oklahoma%21)* and *[The Pajama Game](:The_Pajama_Game)*. Following minor appearances as an understudy in various other productions, MacLaine made her film debut with [Alfred Hitchcock](:Alfred_Hitchcock)'s black comedy *[The Trouble with Harry](:The_Trouble_with_Harry)* (1955), winning the [Golden Globe Award for New Star of the Year – Actress](:Golden_Globe_Award_for_New_Star_of_the_Year_%E2%80%93_Actress). She rose to prominence with starring roles in *[Around the World in 80 Days](:Around_the_World_in_80_Days_%281956_film%29)* (1956), *[Some Came Running](:Some_Came_Running_%28film%29)* (1958), *[Ask Any Girl](:Ask_Any_Girl_%28film%29)* (1959), *[The Apartment](:The_Apartment)* (1960), *[The Children's Hour](:The_Children%27s_Hour_%28film%29)* (1961), *[Irma la Douce](:Irma_la_Douce)* (1963), *[Sweet Charity](:Sweet_Charity_%28film%29)* (1969), and *[Being There](:Being_There)* (1979). A six-time Academy Award nominee, MacLaine won the [Academy Award for Best Actress](:Academy_Award_for_Best_Actress) for the [James L. Brooks](:James_L%2E_Brooks) comedy drama *[Terms of Endearment](:Terms_of_Endearment)* (1983).Her other prominent films include *[The Turning Point](:The_Turning_Point_%281977_film%29)* (1977), *[Madame Sousatzka](:Madame_Sousatzka)* (1988), *[Steel Magnolias](:Steel_Magnolias)* (1989), *[Postcards from the Edge](:Postcards_from_the_Edge_%28film%29)* (1990), *[In Her Shoes](:In_Her_Shoes_%28film%29)* (2005) and *[Bernie](:Bernie_%282011_film%29)* (2011). She portrayed [Coco Chanel](:Coco_Chanel) in the television film, *[Coco Chanel](:Coco_Chanel_%28film%29)* (2008), receiving a [Primetime Emmy Award](:Primetime_Emmy_Award), [Screen Actors Guild Award](:Screen_Actors_Guild_Award), and [Golden Globe Award](:Golden_Globe_Award) nominations.MacLaine has been the recipient of many honorary awards. She was awarded the [AFI Life Achievement Award](:AFI_Life_Achievement_Award) in 2012, Gala Tribute from the [Film Society of Lincoln Center](:Film_Society_of_Lincoln_Center) in 1995, and [Kennedy Center Honor](:Kennedy_Center_Honors) in 2013 for her contribution to American culture, through performing arts. In 1998, she was awarded the [Golden Globe Cecil B. DeMille Award](:Golden_Globe_Cecil_B%2E_DeMille_Award). Apart from acting, MacLaine has written numerous books regarding the subjects of [metaphysics](:Metaphysics), [spirituality](:Spirituality), and [reincarnation](:Reincarnation), as well as a best-selling memoir, *[Out on a Limb](:Out_on_a_Limb_%28book%29)* (1983)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
