<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Drive-Thru_Records> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Drive-Thru Records** was a California-based [independent record label](:Independent_record_label) owned by siblings [Richard](:Richard_Reines) and Stefanie Reines. The label was partially responsible for popularizing the [pop-punk](:Pop-punk)/[emo](:Emo) sound of the early to mid-2000s. After facing financial obstacles in getting their bands' music into stores, Drive-Thru entered into a distribution deal with [MCA](:MCA_Records)/[Universal](:Universal_Records_%281995%29). In 2003 MCA was dissolved and folded into [Geffen Records](:Geffen_Records). One of the clauses of that agreement was MCA/Geffen being able to sign any band of their choosing from Drive-Thru's roster.Once the Universal deal ended, they signed a contract with independent label [Sanctuary Records](:Sanctuary_Records). Starting with a new roster, the label garnered a top 10 platinum hit with \"Here In Your Arms\" by [Hellogoodbye](:Hellogoodbye). However, Sanctuary was on the verge of bankruptcy, and sold their assets to Universal Music. Drive-Thru Records got trapped in that deal, and were stuck with [UMG](:Universal_Music_Group), the catalog division of Universal. The label's last release was in 2008, when Richard and Stefanie Reines decided to concentrate on artist management and put Drive-Thru Records on indefinite hiatus.The independent label sold over 7 million CDs. Bands that achieved notable success during their time with the label include [Hellogoodbye](:Hellogoodbye), [New Found Glory](:New_Found_Glory), [Something Corporate](:Something_Corporate), [Dashboard Confessional](:Dashboard_Confessional), [Fenix\\*TX](:Fenix%2ATX), [Finch](:Finch_%28U%2ES%2E_band%29), [The Starting Line](:The_Starting_Line), [Steel Train](:Steel_Train), [Midtown](:Midtown_%28band%29), [Rx Bandits](:Rx_Bandits), [The Early November](:The_Early_November), [Senses Fail](:Senses_Fail), [Home Grown](:Home_Grown), [Halifax](:Halifax_%28band%29) and [Allister](:Allister)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
