<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ralph_Kemplen> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Ralph Kemplen (8 October 1912 – 4 April 2004) was a British film editor with more than fifty film credits between 1933 and 1982. Kemplen had a long collaboration with director John Huston (1906-1987) on six films between 1951 and 1966. Kemplen also directed one feature film, The Spaniard's Curse (1958). Kemplen won the BAFTA Award for Best Editing for The Day of the Jackal (1973) and was nominated three times for the Academy Award for Best Film Editing (for Moulin Rouge (1952), Oliver! (1968), and Day of the Jackal)."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Jan_Kiesser> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Jan Kiesser is a cinematographer who won a Leo Award for the 2006 film Fido and many other nominations including a Primetime Emmy Award."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Eddy_Joseph> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/birthYear> "1945"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/María_Elena_Marqués> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/The_Lady's_from_Kentucky> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Lady's from Kentucky is a 1939 film directed by Alexander Hall and starring George Raft and Ellen Drew. It was written by Malcolm Stuart Boylan from a story by Rowland Brown. The screenplay involves a failing bookie (Raft) who becomes half owner of a racehorse, with a Kentucky lady (Drew) owning the other half. ZaSu Pitts plays a supporting role."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/The_Bronx> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://dbpedia.org/ontology/CityDistrict> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Nayyar_Sultana> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Nayyar Sultana** (born **Tayyaba Bano**; 1937 – 27 October 1992), sometimes referred to in Pakistan as *Malka-i-Jazbaat* (Queen of Sentiments), was a Pakistani film actress. She became one of the foremost screen actresses of [Lollywood](:Lollywood) in the 1950s and 1960s."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
