<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Zohra_Sehgal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Zohra Mumtaz Sehgal** (born **Sahibzadi Zohra Mumtazullah Khan Begum**; 27 April 1912 – 10 July 2014) was an Indian actress, dancer, and [choreographer](:Choreographer). Having begun her career as a member of a [contemporary dance](:Dance_in_India%23Contemporary_dance) troupe, she transitioned into acting roles beginning in the 1940s. Sehgal appeared in several [British films](:British_film_industry), [television shows](:British_television_shows), and [Bollywood productions](:Bollywood) in a career that spanned over six decades.Sehgal's most notable films include *Neecha Nagar*, *Afsar* (1946), *[Bhaji on the Beach](:Bhaji_on_the_Beach)* (1992), *[The Mystic Masseur](:The_Mystic_Masseur)* (2001), *[Bend It Like Beckham](:Bend_It_Like_Beckham)* (2002), *[Dil Se..](:Dil_Se%2E%2E)* (1998), *[Saaya](:Saaya_%282003_film%29)* (2003), [Veer-Zara](:Veer-Zaara) (2004) *[Saawariya](:Saawariya)* and *[Cheeni Kum](:Cheeni_Kum)* (2007); and the TV serials *[The Jewel in the Crown](:The_Jewel_in_the_Crown_%28TV_series%29)* (1984), *[Tandoori Nights](:Tandoori_Nights)* (1985–87) and *Amma and Family* (1996). At the age of 90, she played the central character in the 2002 film *[Chalo Ishq Ladaaye](:Chalo_Ishq_Ladaaye)*. Considered the doyenne of [Indian theatre](:Indian_theatre), she acted with the [Indian People's Theatre Association](:Indian_People%27s_Theatre_Association) ([IPTA](:Indian_People%27s_Theatre_Association)) and [Prithviraj Kapoor](:Prithviraj_Kapoor)'s [Prithvi Theatre](:Prithvi_Theatre) for 14 years.Sehgal was awarded the [Padma Shri](:Padma_Shri) in 1998, [Kalidas Samman](:Kalidas_Samman) in 2001, and in 2004 the [Sangeet Natak Akademi](:Sangeet_Natak_Akademi) (India's National Academy for Music, Dance and Drama) presented her with its highest award, the [Sangeet Natak Akademi Fellowship](:List_of_Sangeet_Natak_Akademi_fellows) for lifetime achievement. She received the [Padma Vibhushan](:Padma_Vibhushan), India's second-highest civilian honour, in 2010. She died in a [New Delhi](:New_Delhi) hospital on 10 July 2014 due to [cardiac arrest](:Cardiac_arrest)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
