<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Carl_Davis> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Carl Davis**, [CBE](:Commander_of_the_Order_of_the_British_Empire) (born October 28, 1936) is an American-born [conductor](:Conductor_%28music%29) and [composer](:Composer) who has lived in the United Kingdom since 1961.He has written music for more than 100 television programmes, but is best known for creating music to accompany [silent films](:Silent_movie). Davis's music is published by [Faber Music](:Faber_Music)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Adnan_Sami> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Adnan Sami Khan** (born 15 August 1971) is an Indian singer, musician, music composer and pianist. He performs Indian and Western music, including for Hindi, Kannada, [Telugu](:Telugu_Language) and [Tamil](:Tamil_language) movies. He has been awarded with Padma Shri (India's fourth highest civilian award) for his remarkable contribution in music. His most notable instrument is the piano. He has been credited as \"the first musician to have played the [santoor](:Santoor) and Indian classical music on the piano\". A review in the US-based *[Keyboard](:Keyboard_%28magazine%29)* magazine described him as the fastest keyboard player in the world and called him the keyboard discovery of the nineties.He was raised and educated in the United Kingdom and spent his life in Canada. He was born to [Arshad Sami Khan](:Arshad_Sami_Khan), a [Pakistani](:Pakistan) Air Force veteran and diplomat of [Pashtun](:Pashtuns) origin, and Naureen, who was originally from the Indian Union territory of [Jammu and Kashmir](:Jammu_and_Kashmir_%28union_territory%29). *[The Times of India](:The_Times_of_India)* has called him the \"Sultan of Music\". In 2016 he became an Indian citizen. He was awarded the [Padma Shri](:Padma_Shri) on 26 January 2020."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
